      "title": "",
      "transparent": true,
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 3,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "## 1. How the gate decides whether traffic is familiar\nThe five detectors were trained on day 1. K-Means groups the day-1 traffic into 50 clusters. For every new connection the gate measures the distance to the nearest cluster centre. Further than the threshold means **unfamiliar**: the detectors have never seen traffic like it. Pick a connection at the top left to see its verdict."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "type": "xychart",
      "title": "Map of the traffic (55 features flattened to 2)",
      "description": "Each dot is one traffic pattern. Blue is day-1 traffic, which the detectors were trained on. White dots are the 50 cluster centres. Green and red are day-2 traffic the gate calls familiar and unfamiliar. The large yellow dot is the selected connection and the dotted yellow line joins it to its nearest centre. The map squeezes 55 features into 2, so distances on screen are approximate; the verdict on the right uses the real distance.",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 14,
        "w": 16,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 3
      },
      "datasource": {
        "type": "grafana-postgresql-datasource",
        "uid": "ddos-pg"
      },
